AIMED.سجّل({"slug":"gi-physiology","عنوان":"Gastrointestinal Physiology","تمهيد":"","أقسام":[{"معرّف":"part-i-general-principles-of-gastrointestinal-physiology","عنوان":"Part I: General Principles of Gastrointestinal Physiology","html":"<h3 id=\"11-functional-anatomy-overview\">1.1 Functional Anatomy Overview</h3><p>The gastrointestinal (GI) tract is a continuous hollow tube extending from the mouth to the anus, approximately 9 meters in length in the adult human. It is structurally organized into four concentric layers, known as tunics, which are present from the mid-esophagus to the anal canal. From the lumen outward, these are: the mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa (or adventitia in the retroperitoneal segments). Each layer contains specialized cells and structures that subserve the core physiological functions. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The mucosa is the innermost layer, in direct contact with the luminal contents. It is itself composed of three layers: a lining epithelium, the lamina propria, and the muscularis mucosae. The epithelium is a continuous sheet of cells that varies regionally to match function. It is a stratified squamous, protective, non-keratinized epithelium in the mouth, pharynx, and esophagus, where mechanical abrasion is the dominant stress. From the gastric cardia to the anal verge, it transforms into a single-layered columnar epithelium specialized for secretion and absorption. This epithelial monolayer is the critical barrier between the internal milieu and the vast antigenic, microbial, and toxic load of the gut lumen. The epithelium is renewed every 3–6 days by the proliferation of pluripotent stem cells located in the base of the intestinal crypts (of Lieberkühn) and the neck of the gastric glands; this rapid turnover is one of the highest rates of cell division in the body and renders the gut epithelium vulnerable to the antiproliferative effects of chemotherapy and radiation. The lamina propria is the loose connective tissue stroma directly beneath the epithelium. It contains blood and lymphatic capillaries, the enteric nerve terminals, and a large population of resident immune cells (lymphocytes, plasma cells, macrophages, dendritic cells, mast cells, and eosinophils) that constitute the gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), the body&#39;s largest immune organ. The muscularis mucosae is a thin layer of smooth muscle that contracts locally to create small folds and ridges in the mucosa, agitating the unstirred water layer and facilitating the contact of luminal contents with the epithelium. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The submucosa is a dense, irregular fibroelastic connective tissue layer. It contains the larger blood and lymphatic vessels that supply the mucosa, and it houses the submucosal (Meissner&#39;s) plexus, the component of the enteric nervous system primarily concerned with the regulation of mucosal secretion, blood flow, and absorption. In the duodenum, the submucosa contains the Brunner&#39;s glands, which secrete an alkaline, bicarbonate-rich mucus that protects the duodenal epithelium from the acidic gastric chyme. In the esophagus, the submucosa contains the esophageal glands proper, which secrete mucus for lubrication. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The muscularis externa is the major smooth muscle coat responsible for the motor functions of the GI tract. It is composed of two distinct layers: a thick inner circular smooth muscle layer and a thinner outer longitudinal smooth muscle layer. The myenteric (Auerbach&#39;s) plexus is situated between these two muscle layers. It is the primary motor control center of the enteric nervous system, regulating the frequency, velocity, and amplitude of the peristaltic contractions. The coordinated contraction of the circular muscle behind the luminal bolus and its relaxation ahead of the bolus, together with the contraction of the longitudinal muscle that shortens the segment, generates the propagating peristaltic wave that propels the contents aborally (toward the anus). In the stomach, the muscularis externa has a third, innermost oblique muscle layer that is functionally integrated with the circular layer to facilitate the churning and grinding of solid food. In the upper third of the esophagus and the external anal sphincter, the muscle is striated, reflecting the somatic, voluntary control of swallowing initiation and defecation. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The serosa is the outermost layer, a continuous sheet of simple squamous epithelium (mesothelium) that secretes a small volume of serous fluid. This fluid lubricates the surface of the abdominal viscera, allowing the loops of bowel to move and slide against each other and against the parietal peritoneum of the abdominal wall without friction. The GI organs that are retroperitoneal (the duodenum, the ascending and descending colon, the pancreas, and the kidneys) lack a serosa; instead, their outer surface is covered by a fibrous connective tissue adventitia that blends with the surrounding retroperitoneal connective tissue. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The surface area of the small intestine, the primary site of digestion and absorption, is amplified approximately 600-fold by three levels of structural organization, yielding a total absorptive area of approximately 30–40 square meters, roughly the surface area of a doubles tennis court. The first level is the plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring): macroscopic, permanent, crescent-shaped folds of the mucosa and submucosa that project into the lumen. They are most prominent in the distal duodenum and jejunum. They force the luminal chyme to follow a spiral path, which increases the contact time with the absorptive surface. The second level is the villi: finger-like projections of the lamina propria covered by the epithelium, approximately 0.5–1.5 mm in height. Each villus contains a central lymphatic capillary, the lacteal, and a dense capillary network. The third level is the microvilli: the densely packed, uniform, rod-like projections of the apical plasma membrane of each enterocyte. Each enterocyte bears approximately 3000 microvilli. The microvilli constitute the visible brush border on light microscopy. The apical membrane of the microvillus is coated by the glycocalyx, a carbohydrate-rich, fuzzy coat composed of the extracellular domains of the integral membrane glycoproteins and glycolipids. The glycocalyx is the immediate interface between the enterocyte and the luminal environment. It traps a layer of water and mucus, known as the unstirred water layer, through which solutes must diffuse before they contact their specific transport proteins. The brush border contains the terminal digestive enzymes (disaccharidases, peptidases) anchored in the apical membrane, and the specific transporters for the absorbed nutrients. The intestinal crypts (glands) are the invaginations at the base of the villi. They contain the stem cell niche, the proliferating progenitor cells, and the secretory cells (goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"12-the-enteric-nervous-system-ens\">1.2 The Enteric Nervous System (ENS)</h3><p>The enteric nervous system is the intrinsic, independent nervous system of the GI tract. It is a vast, complex network of approximately 200–600 million neurons embedded entirely within the wall of the gut, extending from the esophagus to the internal anal sphincter. It has been described as the &quot;second brain&quot; or the &quot;little brain in the gut.&quot; The ENS can control the GI functions entirely independently of the central nervous system. If all the extrinsic autonomic nerves to the gut are severed, the ENS still generates coordinated, propulsive peristalsis, regulates secretion, and monitors the luminal environment. This intrinsic autonomy is a unique feature that distinguishes the GI tract from all other peripheral organ systems. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The ENS is organized into two major ganglionated plexuses. The myenteric (Auerbach&#39;s) plexus is located between the circular and longitudinal smooth muscle layers of the muscularis externa. It forms a continuous, mesh-like network that runs the entire length of the GI tract. The primary function of the myenteric plexus is the control of GI motility. The neurons of the myenteric plexus regulate the resting tone of the smooth muscle, the frequency and intensity of the rhythmic, segmental contractions, and the initiation, velocity, and propagation of the peristaltic waves. The submucosal (Meissner&#39;s) plexus is located in the submucosa, just deep to the muscularis mucosae. It is less prominent and less extensively interconnected than the myenteric plexus. Its primary function is the regulation of mucosal secretion, absorption, and the local blood flow within the lamina propria and the submucosa. It contains the secretomotor neurons that directly innervate the intestinal crypt cells and the mucosal glands to stimulate the secretion of fluid, electrolytes, and mucus. The two plexuses are interconnected by interganglionic fiber tracts; they function as an integrated system. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The ENS contains a complete set of sensory neurons, interneurons, and motor neurons. The sensory neurons (intrinsic primary afferent neurons, IPANs) have their cell bodies in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses and their receptive endings in the mucosa and the smooth muscle. They detect the chemical composition of the luminal contents (pH, osmolarity, glucose, fatty acids, amino acids, and the presence of specific bacterial products), the mechanical distension of the gut wall, and the tension of the smooth muscle. The interneurons process and integrate this sensory information, forming complex, programmed neural circuits that encode specific motor patterns. The motor neurons are the final common pathway. Excitatory motor neurons release acetylcholine and substance P, which bind to muscarinic M3 receptors and tachykinin NK1 receptors on the smooth muscle, respectively, causing depolarization and contraction of the muscle. Inhibitory motor neurons release nitric oxide, vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP), and pituitary adenylate cyclase-activating polypeptide (PACAP). These mediators hyperpolarize the smooth muscle cell membrane and cause relaxation. The peristaltic reflex—the coordinated contraction of the circular muscle proximal to a bolus and the simultaneous relaxation of the circular muscle distal to the bolus—is entirely hardwired into the ENS circuitry. It does not require any input from the central nervous system. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"13-autonomic-innervation-of-the-gi-tract\">1.3 Autonomic Innervation of the GI Tract</h3><p>Although the ENS can function autonomously, the extrinsic autonomic nervous system modulates and fine-tunes the GI functions. The parasympathetic and sympathetic divisions provide the major extrinsic input. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The parasympathetic innervation of the GI tract is supplied by the vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) and the pelvic nerves (sacral parasympathetic outflow, S2-S4). The vagus nerve innervates the esophagus, stomach, small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The pelvic nerves innervate the distal third of the transverse colon, the descending and sigmoid colon, and the rectum. The parasympathetic nerve fibers are preganglionic, heavily myelinated, and cholinergic. They synapse on the postganglionic neurons of the ENS (both in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses). The vagal and pelvic parasympathetic stimulation generally enhances the activity of the ENS, increasing the force and frequency of peristalsis, promoting secretion, and relaxing the sphincters. This is the neural basis of the cephalic phase of digestion: the sight, smell, thought, and taste of food activate the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus in the medulla, which sends a volley of excitatory impulses to the ENS, preparing the stomach and intestine for the imminent arrival of a meal. The vagus nerve also contains a large population of afferent sensory fibers (80% of the vagal fibers are afferent) that carry information about the luminal chemistry, gut wall distension, and the state of the ENS back to the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) in the brainstem. This vagovagal reflex arc is the fundamental unit of the brain-gut interaction. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The sympathetic innervation arises from the preganglionic neurons in the intermediolateral cell column of the spinal cord from segments T5 to L2. The preganglionic fibers synapse in the prevertebral ganglia (the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric ganglia). The postganglionic noradrenergic fibers then travel along the blood vessels and directly innervate the myenteric and submucosal plexuses, and to a lesser extent the smooth muscle and the mucosa directly. The principal sympathetic neurotransmitter is norepinephrine, acting primarily on alpha-2 adrenergic receptors on the ENS neurons. Activation of the sympathetic nervous system presynaptically inhibits the release of acetylcholine from the excitatory ENS motor neurons, thereby globally suppressing the GI motility, secretion, and blood flow. The splanchnic vasoconstriction, also mediated by the sympathetic nerves (via alpha-1 receptors on the vascular smooth muscle), reduces the blood flow to the GI tract, shunting it to the heart, brain, and skeletal muscle. This is the physiological response to acute stress, severe exercise, or hemorrhagic shock. The sympathetic nervous system also directly innervates the sphincters, causing their contraction (via alpha-1 adrenergic receptors), which reinforces the barrier function and prevents reflux. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"14-gastrointestinal-hormones\">1.4 Gastrointestinal Hormones</h3><p>The GI tract is the largest endocrine organ in the body. Enteroendocrine cells are specialized, polarized, flask-shaped epithelial cells scattered individually among the absorptive enterocytes throughout the mucosa of the stomach and the small and large intestine. They are characterized by the presence of secretory granules in their basal cytoplasm, positioned adjacent to the basolateral membrane and the subepithelial capillaries. The apical surface of the enteroendocrine cell is exposed to the luminal contents and expresses chemosensory receptors (G protein-coupled receptors for nutrients, the GPRs). When specific luminal stimuli (e.g., fatty acids, amino acids, glucose, acid) are detected, the enteroendocrine cell releases its stored peptide hormone from its basal granules into the lamina propria and the bloodstream. The major GI hormones are: <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p><strong>Gastrin:</strong> Synthesized and secreted by the G cells located in the antral (pyloric) mucosa of the stomach. The primary stimulus for gastrin release is the presence of partially digested protein (peptides and amino acids, especially phenylalanine and tryptophan) in the gastric lumen. The distension of the antrum and the vagal stimulation (via gastrin-releasing peptide, GRP) also stimulate the G cells. The release is inhibited by somatostatin from the antral D cells, which is itself stimulated by a luminal pH &lt; 3.0; this negative feedback loop is the primary mechanism that terminates the gastric phase of acid secretion. Gastrin&#39;s primary physiological action is the stimulation of gastric acid secretion by the parietal cells. It does this both directly (by binding to the CCK-B receptor on the parietal cell, increasing intracellular calcium) and, more significantly, indirectly by stimulating the histamine-producing enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in the gastric corpus. Gastrin is also a trophic hormone; it stimulates the proliferation and growth of the gastric mucosa (especially the parietal cells and ECL cells) and the colonic mucosa. Pathological, unregulated hypergastrinemia, as in a gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome), causes profound gastric acid hypersecretion, severe, refractory peptic ulcer disease, and the hypertrophy of the gastric rugae. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p><strong>Cholecystokinin (CCK):</strong> Synthesized and secreted by the I cells located in the mucosa of the duodenum and the jejunum. The primary, potent stimulus for CCK release is the presence of fatty acids and monoglycerides in the duodenal lumen. Amino acids also stimulate its release, but to a lesser degree. CCK has four major physiological actions, all coordinating the digestion and absorption of dietary fat. (1) It stimulates the gallbladder smooth muscle to contract and, simultaneously, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, delivering the concentrated bile into the duodenum to emulsify the fat. (2) It stimulates the pancreatic acinar cells to secrete the digestive enzymes (the zymogens). (3) It slows gastric emptying; this is the duodenal brake. The fat in the duodenum triggers a powerful CCK-mediated inhibition of the gastric antral contractions and an increase in the pyloric tone. This slows the delivery of the gastric chyme, preventing the duodenum from being overwhelmed by a large, hyperosmolar, fatty meal load. (4) CCK is a satiety signal. It acts on the CCK-A receptors on the vagal afferent nerve terminals in the duodenum and on the brainstem, signaling the brain that a meal has been consumed and is being processed. This promotes meal termination. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p><strong>Secretin:</strong> Synthesized and secreted by the S cells in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa. The primary, and essentially the only, stimulus for secretin release is the presence of acid (a pH &lt; 4.5) in the duodenal lumen. Secretin is the physiological antagonist of the gastric acid. Its single most important action is the stimulation of the pancreatic ductal (centroacinar and ductal) cells to secrete a large volume of a bicarbonate-rich, isotonic fluid. This bicarbonate-rich fluid enters the duodenum and chemically neutralizes the acidic gastric chyme, raising the luminal pH to the optimal range (pH 6-7) for the pancreatic digestive enzymes to function. Secretin also reduces gastric acid secretion (by inhibiting gastrin release from the antral G cells) and gastric motility. It is a component of the negative feedback that ensures the duodenal mucosa is protected from acid-peptic injury. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p><strong>Gastric Inhibitory Polypeptide (GIP), also called Glucose-dependent Insulinotropic Polypeptide:</strong> Synthesized and secreted by the K cells in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa. It is released in response to the presence of glucose and fat in the lumen. GIP has two physiological functions. Its primary, endocrine action is the augmentation of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion from the pancreatic beta cells. This is the incretin effect: an oral glucose load provokes a significantly larger insulin response than an equivalent intravenous glucose load, because the oral glucose triggers the release of the incretin hormones (GIP and GLP-1) from the gut, which prime the beta cell to secrete more insulin. The second action of GIP is the inhibition of gastric acid secretion and gastric emptying (though this is weaker than the incretin effect). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p><strong>Glucagon-Like Peptide-1 (GLP-1):</strong> Synthesized from the proglucagon gene by the post-translational processing by prohormone convertase 1/3, primarily in the L cells of the distal ileum and the colon. GLP-1 is released in response to the arrival of nutrients (glucose and fat) in the distal small intestine and the colon. It is the other major incretin hormone, potentiating the glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (the incretin effect). In addition, GLP-1 powerfully inhibits gastric emptying, reducing the postprandial glycemic excursion. It also inhibits glucagon secretion from the pancreatic alpha cells, further reducing the hepatic glucose output. GLP-1 promotes satiety by acting on the hypothalamus. The GLP-1 receptor agonists (e.g., semaglutide, liraglutide, tirzepatide) are a revolutionary therapeutic class for type 2 diabetes and obesity, precisely because they exploit these multiple physiological mechanisms. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p><strong>Motilin:</strong> Synthesized by the M cells in the duodenal and jejunal mucosa. Its release is not triggered by a meal, but rather by the fasting state and the cyclical neural activity of the ENS. Motilin is the endocrine initiator of Phase III of the migrating motor complex (MMC), the interdigestive, housekeeping wave of peristalsis. Motilin levels in the blood peak approximately every 90–120 minutes in the fasting state, and each peak coincides with the initiation of a Phase III gastric contraction wave. Motilin acts on the motilin receptors on the gastric smooth muscle and the cholinergic ENS neurons. Erythromycin and other macrolide antibiotics are potent, non-peptide motilin receptor agonists, which explains their powerful gastric prokinetic effect. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p><strong>Ghrelin:</strong> Synthesized predominantly by the X/A-like cells of the gastric oxyntic mucosa (the fundus). Ghrelin is the only known orexigenic (appetite-stimulating) gut hormone. Its secretion is highest during fasting, increases just before a meal, and is rapidly suppressed by food intake (especially by protein and glucose). The name ghrelin is derived from the Proto-Indo-European root &quot;ghre&quot; meaning &quot;to grow,&quot; reflecting its second function: it is a potent stimulator of growth hormone release from the anterior pituitary (via the growth hormone secretagogue receptor, GHS-R). Ghrelin acts on the NPY/AgRP neurons in the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus to stimulate hunger and food intake. It also promotes gastric motility and acid secretion, preparing the stomach for the expected meal. Ghrelin levels are low in obesity and high in cachexia and starvation. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"15-electrical-activity-of-gi-smooth-muscle\">1.5 Electrical Activity of GI Smooth Muscle</h3><p>The smooth muscle of the GI tract is a unitary (single-unit, visceral) smooth muscle. The individual smooth muscle cells are electrically coupled by gap junctions (connexin proteins), which form low-resistance pathways for the flow of ions. Consequently, an electrical event initiated in one cell spreads rapidly in a decremental, wave-like fashion to a large, functionally coordinated sheet of muscle. This electrical syncytium ensures that the muscle contracts in a coordinated, rhythmic manner. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The resting membrane potential of the GI smooth muscle is not stable. It undergoes spontaneous, rhythmic, subthreshold depolarization-repolarization cycles known as slow waves (or the basic electrical rhythm, BER). The slow waves are generated by the interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC), which are specialized, non-contractile, mesenchymal-derived, pacemaker cells that are interposed between the smooth muscle cells and the nerve terminals of the ENS. The ICCs have the unique property of generating a spontaneous, rhythmic inward current. The molecular pacemaker mechanism is the cyclical release of calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum (via the inositol trisphosphate receptor, IP3R), which triggers the opening of the calcium-activated chloride channel ANO1 (anoctamin-1, also known as TMEM16A) on the ICC plasma membrane. The efflux of chloride (or in some contexts the influx of sodium through the non-selective cation channel TRPM7) generates an inward current that depolarizes the ICC membrane. The depolarization spreads passively to the coupled smooth muscle cells via gap junctions. The slow wave frequency is determined by the intrinsic frequency of the ICC pacemaker and is characteristic of each region of the GI tract: 3 cycles per minute in the gastric corpus and antrum, 12 per minute in the duodenum, approximately 9 per minute in the ileum, and a complex, lower frequency of 3–6 per minute in the colon. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The slow wave itself does not cause the smooth muscle to contract. It is a subthreshold event; the peak depolarization of the slow wave normally does not reach the threshold for the opening of the L-type, voltage-gated calcium channels. Contraction (the mechanical event) occurs only when action potentials (spike potentials) are superimposed on the peak of the slow wave. The action potentials are triggered when the membrane potential reaches the threshold of approximately -40 mV. The rising phase of the action potential is carried by the influx of calcium ions through the L-type calcium channels (rather than sodium, as in the nerve and skeletal muscle). This calcium influx directly activates the contractile apparatus. The excitatory neurotransmitters (acetylcholine, substance P) and hormones (gastrin, motilin) increase the plateau amplitude of the slow wave, bringing it closer to the threshold, and increasing the number of action potentials fired during each slow wave cycle, thereby increasing the force of contraction. The inhibitory neurotransmitters (nitric oxide, VIP) hyperpolarize the membrane, moving it further from the threshold, and reducing or abolishing the spike activity and the contraction. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The slow wave frequency sets the maximum possible frequency of the rhythmic contractions in that segment. Because the slow wave is a deterministic electrical oscillator, the phasic contractions of the gut (the rhythmic, segmental contractions) are locked to the slow wave rhythm. In the normal fasting state, the slow waves may not reach the threshold, and there is little to no contractile activity. After a meal, the excitatory neurohumoral environment increases the plateau amplitude, and every slow wave triggers a burst of action potentials and a contraction. This is the maximal contractile response; the frequency is fixed by the slow wave. Tonic contractions, by contrast, are sustained contractions that are not dependent on the slow waves and are generated by the continuous entry of calcium into the smooth muscle cell, often in response to a sustained depolarizing stimulus (e.g., in the sphincters). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"16-neurotransmitters-and-paracrine-mediators\">1.6 Neurotransmitters and Paracrine Mediators</h3><p>The GI smooth muscle and the secretory cells are controlled by a complex interplay of the neurotransmitters released from the ENS neurons and the autonomic nerves, the paracrine mediators released from the enteroendocrine cells and the immune cells, and the circulating endocrine hormones. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p><strong>Acetylcholine (ACh):</strong> The primary excitatory neurotransmitter of the GI tract. It is released from the preganglionic parasympathetic neurons (acting on nicotinic receptors on the ENS neurons), the postganglionic parasympathetic neurons, and the excitatory motor neurons of the ENS. ACh acts on the M3 muscarinic receptor on the smooth muscle (causing contraction), on the M1 receptor on the ENS neurons, and on the M3 receptor on the parietal cell and the pancreatic acinar cell (stimulating secretion).</p><p><strong>Substance P:</strong> A tachykinin neuropeptide that is co-released with ACh from the excitatory motor neurons. It binds to the NK1 receptor on the smooth muscle, causing a slow, sustained depolarization and contraction. It is a key neurotransmitter in the ascending excitatory limb of the peristaltic reflex.</p><p><strong>Nitric Oxide (NO):</strong> The primary inhibitory neurotransmitter of the GI tract. It is synthesized on demand by the neuronal nitric oxide synthase (nNOS) in the inhibitory motor neurons. NO diffuses across the synaptic cleft and into the smooth muscle cell, where it activates the soluble guanylate cyclase, generating cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP). cGMP activates protein kinase G, which opens potassium channels, hyperpolarizes the membrane, and reduces the intracellular calcium concentration, causing profound relaxation. NO is the mediator of the descending inhibitory limb of the peristaltic reflex. The loss of the nitrergic inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus is the fundamental pathological lesion in achalasia (failure of the LES to relax) and is also implicated in the pathogenesis of the diabetic gastroparesis and the hypertrophic pyloric stenosis of infants.</p><p><strong>Vasoactive Intestinal Polypeptide (VIP):</strong> A 28-amino-acid peptide that is co-released with NO from the inhibitory motor neurons. VIP binds to its G protein-coupled receptor (VPAC1) on the smooth muscle, activating adenylate cyclase, increasing cAMP, and causing relaxation. VIP is a major mediator of the receptive relaxation of the gastric fundus and the intestinal secretion. VIPomas are rare neuroendocrine tumors that secrete excessive VIP, causing the WDHA syndrome (Watery Diarrhea, Hypokalemia, Achlorhydria).</p><p><strong>Serotonin (5-Hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT):</strong> More than 90% of the body&#39;s serotonin is located in the enterochromaffin (EC) cells of the intestinal mucosa. 5-HT is the key paracrine signal that initiates the peristaltic reflex. When the luminal pressure increases or the mucosa is mechanically stroked, the EC cells release 5-HT. 5-HT binds to the 5-HT3 and 5-HT4 receptors on the mucosal processes of the intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs), activating the sensory limb of the ENS. The 5-HT3 receptor antagonists (ondansetron) cause constipation, and the 5-HT4 receptor agonists (prucalopride) are prokinetic.</p><p><strong>Histamine:</strong> In the gastric mucosa, histamine is stored in and released from the enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in response to gastrin and ACh. Histamine diffuses across the interstitial space and binds to the H2 receptor on the basolateral membrane of the neighboring parietal cell, activating adenylate cyclase and generating cAMP, which is the most potent stimulus for the activation of the H+/K+-ATPase (the proton pump). The H2 receptor antagonists (famotidine) are competitive antagonists of this pathway.</p><p><strong>Somatostatin:</strong> A 14-amino-acid peptide that is the universal paracrine inhibitory hormone of the GI tract. It is produced by the D cells in the gastric antrum, the gastric corpus, and the pancreatic islets (delta cells). Somatostatin inhibits the secretion of gastrin from the G cells, histamine from the ECL cells, acid from the parietal cell, and the secretion of the pancreatic enzymes and bicarbonate. It also inhibits the motility and the splanchnic blood flow. Its release is stimulated by the luminal acid. Octreotide is a long-acting, synthetic somatostatin analogue.</p>"},{"معرّف":"part-ii-motility","عنوان":"Part II: Motility","html":"<h3 id=\"21-mastication-and-swallowing\">2.1 Mastication and Swallowing</h3><p>Mastication (chewing) is a voluntary, highly coordinated neuromuscular act involving the muscles of the jaw, tongue, and cheeks. It is controlled by the motor neurons of the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V, for the muscles of mastication: masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids), the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII, for the buccinator and the lips), and the hypoglossal nerve (cranial nerve XII, for the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue). The act of chewing is initiated voluntarily by the primary motor cortex, but the rhythmic, grinding, repetitive movements of the mandible are generated by a central pattern generator in the brainstem, the masticatory rhythm generator. The functions of mastication are to mechanically break down the large food particles into smaller, swallowable fragments (reducing the particle size from approximately 2 cm to &lt;2 mm), to mix the food with the saliva (forming a lubricated bolus), and to initiate the enzymatic digestion of the starches by the salivary alpha-amylase. The contact of the food with the taste buds on the tongue stimulates a volley of gustatory afferent signals (via the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve and the glossopharyngeal nerve) that reflexively enhances the salivary secretion, the gastric acid secretion, and the pancreatic secretion (the cephalic phase). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>Swallowing (deglutition) is a complex, stereotyped, all-or-none, programmed motor sequence that transfers the food bolus from the pharynx to the stomach. It is controlled by the swallowing center, a diffuse network of interconnected neurons in the medulla oblongata (the nucleus tractus solitarius, the nucleus ambiguus, and the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus). The swallowing center receives the afferent sensory input from the pharyngeal and laryngeal mechanoreceptors and taste receptors (via the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves), and it coordinates the sequential, precisely timed activation and inhibition of the motor neurons to the striated muscles of the pharynx, larynx, and the upper esophagus. The reflex is divided into three phases. The oral phase is voluntary. The tongue pushes the bolus posteriorly against the hard palate and into the pharynx. The bolus entering the pharynx triggers the involuntary pharyngeal phase. This is an irreversible, reflex sequence that lasts approximately 1 second. The soft palate elevates and tightly opposes the posterior pharyngeal wall, sealing off the nasopharynx and preventing the nasal regurgitation. The larynx is pulled upward and forward by the contraction of the suprahyoid muscles, which simultaneously opens the upper esophageal sphincter. The epiglottis folds posteriorly over the laryngeal inlet. The vocal cords adduct, and the respiration is reflexively inhibited (swallowing apnea, a brief interruption of the respiratory rhythm that protects against aspiration). The upper esophageal sphincter (UES), which is the cricopharyngeus muscle and the adjacent fibers of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor, relaxes and is pulled open by the anterior movement of the larynx. The pharyngeal constrictor muscles (superior, middle, and inferior) contract sequentially, generating a peristaltic wave of pressure that propels the bolus through the hypopharynx and into the proximal esophagus. The esophageal phase begins as the bolus passes through the UES. The UES then closes, preventing the reflux of the esophageal contents back into the pharynx. A primary peristaltic wave, initiated by the swallowing center via the vagal efferents to the esophageal smooth muscle, sweeps down the esophageal body at a velocity of approximately 2–4 cm/s. The wave takes approximately 8–10 seconds to travel the full length of the esophagus. As the peristaltic wave approaches the gastroesophageal junction, the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) undergoes a vagally mediated receptive relaxation, and the bolus is delivered into the stomach. If the primary peristaltic wave fails to clear the esophagus of the bolus, the residual distension triggers a secondary peristaltic wave, which is a local, intrinsic ENS reflex, without an associated pharyngeal phase. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"22-esophageal-motility\">2.2 Esophageal Motility</h3><p>The esophagus is a muscular tube, approximately 25 cm in length, whose sole motor function is the rapid, unidirectional transport of the swallowed bolus from the pharynx to the stomach. The upper third of the esophagus is composed of striated muscle; the middle third is a mixture of striated and smooth muscle; and the lower third is entirely smooth muscle. The UES is a 2–3 cm zone of high pressure (50–100 mmHg) at the pharyngoesophageal junction. It is tonically contracted between swallows, maintaining a barrier that prevents the entry of air into the esophagus during inspiration and the reflux of esophageal contents into the pharynx. The LES is a 3–4 cm zone of tonically contracted, thickened, specialized circular smooth muscle at the gastroesophageal junction. The LES maintains a resting pressure of 15–30 mmHg above the intragastric pressure, creating a high-pressure zone that is the primary anatomical and physiological barrier against the reflux of the acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. The LES tone is maintained by the intrinsic, myogenic properties of the muscle and by the tonic, cholinergic, excitatory input from the ENS. The LES relaxation during swallowing is mediated by the release of nitric oxide and VIP from the inhibitory ENS motor neurons. Transient lower esophageal sphincter relaxations (TLESRs) are spontaneous, prolonged (10–45 seconds), non-swallow-related relaxations of the LES that are the dominant mechanism of the physiological gastroesophageal reflux. TLESRs are triggered by the distension of the gastric fundus (especially the proximal stomach, near the cardia) after a meal and are mediated by a vagovagal reflex. The afferent limb is the vagal mechanoreceptors in the gastric wall; the brainstem integration center is the nucleus tractus solitarius and the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus; and the efferent limb is the inhibitory motor neurons to the LES. TLESRs are the mechanism by which the stomach vents the swallowed air (belching). In GERD, the frequency of the TLESRs is not necessarily increased, but the proportion of the TLESRs that are accompanied by an acid reflux event is abnormally high, due to a disrupted esophagogastric junction (hiatal hernia), an increased gastric pressure (obesity), or an increased compliance of the LES. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"23-gastric-motility\">2.3 Gastric Motility</h3><p>The stomach has three distinct motor regions that are functionally integrated to accomplish the reception, storage, mixing, grinding, and the controlled, regulated emptying of the ingested meal. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The proximal stomach (the fundus and the upper body) is thin-walled and has a low-amplitude, tonic motor activity. Its primary motor function is receptive relaxation: the accommodation of the ingested meal without a significant increase in the intragastric pressure. As the food bolus enters the esophagus, a vagovagal reflex is triggered, causing the release of NO and VIP from the inhibitory ENS neurons in the gastric wall, which relax the fundic smooth muscle. This receptive relaxation allows the stomach to accept a volume of 800–1500 mL with a minimal rise in the intraluminal pressure (&lt;5 mmHg). The fundus also undergoes a slow, sustained, tonic contraction that pushes the food from the fundus into the distal stomach (gastric emptying of liquids is largely driven by the fundic tone and the gastroduodenal pressure gradient). The loss of this normal fundic accommodation—the inability of the fundus to relax adequately, leading to an abnormally rapid increase in the intragastric pressure during the meal—is the pathophysiological hallmark of the functional dyspepsia syndrome (postprandial distress syndrome) and is the basis for the symptom of early satiety. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The distal stomach (the lower body, the antrum, and the pylorus) is thick-walled and generates powerful, rhythmic, phasic contractions at the frequency of 3 cycles per minute, the native gastric slow wave rhythm. The antral contractions are vigorous, peristaltic waves that constrict the lumen nearly to the point of occlusion. As the wave approaches the pylorus, the pylorus contracts, closing the outlet. The solid food particles are propelled against the closed pylorus and are retropulsed backward into the body of the stomach (the antral systole). This grinding (trituration) and the retropulsion reduce the solid food particles to a semiliquid suspension of particles &lt;1–2 mm in diameter. Only particles smaller than 2 mm, along with the liquid phase, are permitted to pass through the pylorus into the duodenum. The rate of gastric emptying is the precisely regulated outcome of the balance between the propulsive forces of the proximal gastric tone and the antral contractions, and the resistance provided by the pylorus and the duodenal brake. The duodenal brake is a powerful inhibitory feedback. The presence of fat, acid, hyperosmolar solutions, and amino acids in the duodenum triggers the release of CCK, secretin, GIP, and GLP-1, and activates the duodenal vagal afferents, which reflexively inhibit the gastric antral contractions, increase the pyloric tone, and reduce the fundic tone. This slows the delivery of the chyme into the duodenum, ensuring that the rate of entry does not exceed the rate at which the duodenum can neutralize the acid and the pancreas and the small intestine can digest and absorb the nutrients. The gastric emptying of liquids is rapid and follows an exponential decay curve (half-time approximately 12–20 minutes). The gastric emptying of solids is slower and has a characteristic biphasic pattern: a lag phase of approximately 20–40 minutes, during which the solids are ground and triturated, followed by a linear phase of emptying. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"24-small-intestinal-motility\">2.4 Small Intestinal Motility</h3><p>The small intestinal motility in the fed state (after a meal) is dominated by the segmentation contractions. These are localized, rhythmic, non-propulsive contractions of the circular muscle that divide the small intestine into small, adjacent segments. The segments then relax, and new contractions form new segments, producing a &quot;chopping&quot; and mixing effect. The frequency of the segmentations is determined by the local slow wave frequency: 12 per minute in the jejunum and 8–9 per minute in the ileum. The primary function of the segmentation is to mix the chyme with the pancreatic enzymes and the bile, and to bring the digested products into intimate contact with the absorptive epithelium, maximizing the absorption. The mixing also keeps the chyme in continuous contact with the mucosa, minimizing the thickness of the unstirred water layer. The peristaltic contractions in the small intestine are short-segment waves that propel the chyme a few centimeters in the aboral direction. They occur at a slower frequency than the segmentations. The net aboral transit is slow, taking approximately 3–5 hours for the chyme to travel from the duodenum to the ileocecal valve. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The migrating motor complex (MMC) is the distinct, organized, cyclical motor pattern that occurs in the stomach and the small intestine during the fasting state (when the meal has been completely emptied from the stomach and the small intestine, approximately 4–6 hours after a meal). The MMC consists of four phases that repeat every 90–120 minutes. Phase I is a period of complete quiescence (no contractions, approximately 40–60 minutes). Phase II is a period of irregular, intermittent, low-amplitude contractions (approximately 20–40 minutes). Phase III is a sudden, discrete burst of high-amplitude, rhythmic, peristaltic contractions at the maximal frequency allowed by the slow wave (3/min in the stomach, 12/min in the duodenum). The Phase III wavefront begins in the gastric antrum (or the proximal duodenum) and migrates slowly down the entire length of the small intestine, at a velocity of approximately 5 cm/min, taking approximately 90–120 minutes to reach the terminal ileum. Phase IV is a brief transition back to quiescence. The physiological function of the MMC is the &quot;intestinal housekeeper.&quot; The Phase III contractions sweep the residual, undigested solid debris, the desquamated epithelial cells, the secretions, and the bacteria from the stomach and the small intestine into the colon. The failure of the MMC is the primary pathophysiological mechanism of the small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO). Phase III is initiated by the peptide hormone motilin, released from the M cells in the duodenal mucosa. The motilin peaks in the blood precisely coincide with the Phase III waves. Erythromycin, as a motilin receptor agonist, stimulates a premature, forceful Phase III wave, accelerating the gastric emptying and the small bowel clearance. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The ileocecal valve (sphincter) is a specialized, tonically contracted segment at the junction of the terminal ileum and the cecum. Its functions are to prolong the contact of the ileal chyme with the mucosa, facilitating the absorption of the remaining nutrients, the vitamin B12-intrinsic factor complex, and the bile acids, and to prevent the reflux of the bacteria-laden colonic contents into the sterile small intestine. The ileocecal valve relaxes when a peristaltic wave from the ileum propels the chyme into the cecum; the distension of the cecum then reflexively increases the ileocecal valve tone. The loss of the ileocecal valve (by a right hemicolectomy) allows the colonic bacteria to reflux into the ileum, predisposing to SIBO and the malabsorption. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"25-colonic-motility\">2.5 Colonic Motility</h3><p>The colon, or the large intestine, has motor functions that are fundamentally different from those of the small intestine. The colon has two major physiological functions: (1) the absorption of water and electrolytes (approximately 1400 mL of the 1500 mL of the ileal effluent that enters the cecum each day is absorbed, reducing the stool volume to approximately 100 mL), and (2) the temporary storage and the controlled, periodic evacuation of the solid waste. The colonic motility accomplishes these functions by mixing the colonic contents to maximize the contact with the mucosa, and by slowly propelling the contents aborally. The colon has no villi. The smooth muscle of the colon is organized into the inner circular layer, and the outer longitudinal layer is concentrated into three separate bands, the teniae coli. The nerve supply is the ENS (the myenteric plexus) and the pelvic parasympathetic nerves. The slow wave frequency of the colon is lower and more irregular than the small intestine, at approximately 3–6 cycles per minute. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The predominant motor pattern of the colon is the haustral contractions (segmentation). The circular muscle contracts in discrete, ring-like segments, generating the haustra, the sacculations visible on a barium enema or a colonoscopy. The haustral contractions are non-propulsive; they mix and knead the colonic contents, exposing them to the mucosa for the maximum water and electrolyte absorption. The haustral contractions are responsible for the prolonged colonic transit time (24–48 hours). High-amplitude propagating contractions (HAPCs, also called the mass movements) are a distinct, powerful, propulsive motor pattern. The HAPCs are high-amplitude, prolonged, peristaltic contractions that sweep the luminal contents from the transverse colon into the sigmoid and the rectum. They occur 6–10 times per day, predominantly upon awakening and after a meal. The postprandial HAPCs are the result of the gastrocolic reflex, which is mediated by the gastric distension and the release of gastrin and CCK, which stimulate the colonic motor activity. The HAPCs are the mechanism that delivers the stool to the rectum, initiating the conscious urge to defecate. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"26-defecation\">2.6 Defecation</h3><p>The storage and the controlled evacuation of the stool are the functions of the rectum and the anal canal. The rectum is a distensible reservoir. When the stool enters the rectum from the sigmoid colon, the rectal wall distends. The rectum accommodates the increasing volume with a minimal increase in the intrarectal pressure. The anal canal is the terminal 3–4 cm of the GI tract. It is surrounded by the anal sphincter complex. The internal anal sphincter (IAS) is a thickening of the circular smooth muscle of the rectum, under the involuntary, autonomic control. The IAS is tonically contracted, providing 70–80% of the resting anal tone, which maintains the anal closure and the continence for gas and liquid stool. The external anal sphincter (EAS) is a ring of striated muscle, under the voluntary, somatic control via the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). The EAS provides the squeeze pressure, the voluntary, augmented anal closure during acute urgency. The puborectalis muscle, a component of the levator ani, forms a U-shaped sling that loops posteriorly around the anorectal junction. Its tonic contraction maintains the anorectal angle at approximately 80–90 degrees, creating a mechanical flap valve that contributes significantly to the passive continence. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The defecation reflex is initiated by the arrival of stool in the rectum. The rectal distension triggers the rectoanal inhibitory reflex (RAIR): the intrinsic ENS neurons in the rectal wall cause a transient relaxation of the internal anal sphincter. This allows a small sample of the rectal contents to descend and contact the sensitive, specialized, sensory epithelium of the upper anal canal (the anal transition zone), which discriminates between solid, liquid, and gas. The cortical awareness of this sensation constitutes the conscious urge to defecate. If the circumstances are socially appropriate, the individual voluntarily adopts a sitting or squatting posture. This straightens the anorectal angle. The individual voluntarily relaxes the puborectalis muscle and the external anal sphincter. The intra-abdominal pressure is raised by a Valsalva maneuver (the contraction of the diaphragm and the abdominal muscles against a closed glottis), and the rectal smooth muscle contracts. The combined increase in the intra-abdominal and intrarectal pressure, against the relaxed sphincters, evacuates the stool. If the defecation is deferred, the external anal sphincter is voluntarily contracted, the rectum relaxes to accommodate the stool, and the urge subsides until the next mass movement. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The normal defecation requires the coordinated, reciprocal, and synergistic action of the rectum, the anal sphincters, and the pelvic floor. Dyssynergic defecation is a functional disorder in which the patient paradoxically contracts, or fails to relax, the puborectalis muscle and the external anal sphincter during the attempted defecation. This obstructs the outflow, causing severe, chronic constipation. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p>"},{"معرّف":"part-iii-secretion","عنوان":"Part III: Secretion","html":"<h3 id=\"31-salivary-secretion\">3.1 Salivary Secretion</h3><p>Saliva is produced by three paired major salivary glands—the parotid, the submandibular, and the sublingual—and numerous minor salivary glands distributed throughout the oral mucosa. The total daily secretion is approximately 1000–1500 mL. The parotid glands are purely serous, secreting a watery fluid rich in alpha-amylase. The submandibular glands are mixed sero-mucous, producing both the enzyme-rich serous fluid and the mucin-rich viscous secretion. The sublingual glands are predominantly mucous. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The formation of saliva is a two-stage process. First, the acinar cells secrete a primary secretion that is an ultrafiltrate of plasma, isotonic with plasma (approximately 300 mOsm/L) and similar in ionic composition. The secretion is driven by the active, transcellular transport of chloride ions, which creates an osmotic gradient that draws water and sodium paracellularly into the acinar lumen. Second, as this primary secretion flows through the salivary ducts (the intercalated and striated ducts), the ductal epithelial cells modify the ionic composition. The ductal cells actively reabsorb sodium and chloride from the lumen, and they actively secrete potassium and bicarbonate into the lumen. However, the ductal epithelium is relatively water-impermeable. The net reabsorption of the sodium chloride exceeds the secretion of the potassium bicarbonate, so the final saliva that enters the mouth is hypotonic (approximately 100 mOsm/L) and has a high potassium and bicarbonate concentration. At low flow rates (basal, unstimulated secretion), the saliva spends more time in the ducts, and the ion modification is extensive; the saliva is very hypotonic, low in sodium, and high in potassium. At high flow rates (maximal stimulation, as during a meal), the saliva transits the ducts rapidly, the ion modification is reduced, and the saliva approaches the plasma tonicity, with a higher sodium and chloride concentration. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The control of salivary secretion is exclusively neural. There is no hormonal regulation. The parasympathetic nervous system is the primary driver. The parasympathetic preganglionic neurons originate in the superior salivatory nucleus (facial nerve, cranial nerve VII, via the chorda tympani, to the submandibular and sublingual glands) and the inferior salivatory nucleus (glossopharyngeal nerve, cranial nerve IX, via the otic ganglion, to the parotid gland). The postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release acetylcholine, which binds to the M3 muscarinic receptors on the acinar cells, activating the inositol trisphosphate (IP3) pathway and increasing the intracellular calcium, driving a large volume of watery, enzyme-rich secretion. The parasympathetic stimulation also profoundly increases the submucosal blood flow to the glands, mediated by the release of VIP and the generation of the potent vasodilator kallikrein, which cleaves the circulating kininogen to bradykinin. The sympathetic innervation from the superior cervical ganglion releases norepinephrine, which binds to the beta-adrenergic receptors on the acinar cells, activating the cAMP pathway and stimulating the secretion of a small volume of a viscous, mucin-rich, proteinaceous saliva. The cephalic phase (the sight, smell, thought of food), the gustatory stimulation (taste, especially sour and sweet), and the mechanical stimulation of the oral mucosa (chewing) are the physiological stimuli for the salivary secretion. The conditioned salivary reflex (Pavlov&#39;s dogs) is a classic, learned, cortical association. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The functions of saliva are multiple and vital. The lubrication and protection of the oral mucosa (the mucins, especially MUC5B and MUC7) facilitate the speech and the swallowing. The salivary alpha-amylase initiates the starch digestion. The lingual lipase is acid-stable and continues to digest the triglycerides in the stomach. The saliva is the vehicle for the taste: the tastants must be dissolved in the saliva to reach the taste receptor cells in the taste buds. The saliva is the critical innate defense of the oral cavity. It contains the lysozyme (which cleaves the peptidoglycan cell wall of the Gram-positive bacteria), the lactoferrin (which chelates the iron, starving the iron-dependent bacteria), the secretory IgA (the primary adaptive immune defense of the mucosa), and the histatins and the defensins (antimicrobial peptides). The saliva is supersaturated with calcium and phosphate, which maintains the mineral integrity of the teeth and promotes the remineralization of the early, incipient carious lesions. The bicarbonate in the saliva neutralizes the acid produced by the oral bacteria after a carbohydrate meal, protecting the enamel from the acid-induced demineralization. The loss of the salivary function (xerostomia, as in Sjögren&#39;s syndrome or after the head and neck radiation) causes a devastating cascade of rampant dental caries, oral candidiasis, dysgeusia, dysphagia, and a severely impaired quality of life. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"32-gastric-secretion\">3.2 Gastric Secretion</h3><p>The stomach secretes approximately 2000–3000 mL of gastric juice per day. The gastric juice is a complex mixture of hydrochloric acid, pepsinogen, intrinsic factor, mucus, bicarbonate, and water, each produced by a specific cell type in the gastric glands (the oxyntic glands) in the fundus and body of the stomach. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p>The gastric pit is the opening of the gastric gland onto the surface. The gland is a straight, tube-like structure. The upper region, the isthmus and the neck, contains the mucous neck cells and the stem cells. The mid-region of the gland contains the parietal cells. The base of the gland contains the chief cells and the enteroendocrine cells (ECL cells and D cells). The surface epithelium and the pit cells are the surface mucous cells. They secrete a thick, continuous, tenacious, adherent layer of mucus (primarily the MUC5AC glycoprotein) and bicarbonate. The mucus gel, 50–200 µm thick, is impermeable to pepsin (the mucin glycoproteins are resistant to the proteolytic digestion) and forms an unstirred water layer. The bicarbonate secreted by the surface mucous cells is trapped within the mucus gel and maintains a neutral pH (pH 7) at the epithelial cell surface, despite the luminal pH of 1–2. This mucus-bicarbonate barrier is the first line of the gastric mucosal defense. The prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2), produced by the COX-1 enzyme in the gastric mucosa, are the essential endogenous stimulants of the mucus and bicarbonate secretion and the mucosal blood flow. The NSAIDs inhibit the COX-1, depleting the prostaglandins, and breaking the barrier. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The chief (peptic) cells synthesize and secrete pepsinogen. The pepsinogen is packaged in the zymogen granules and released by the regulated exocytosis in response to the same stimuli that drive the acid secretion (ACh, gastrin, and histamine). Upon contact with the acid in the gastric lumen (pH &lt; 5), the pepsinogen undergoes an autocatalytic, intramolecular cleavage to form the active pepsin. Pepsin is an aspartic endopeptidase with a very acidic pH optimum (1.8–3.5). It initiates the protein digestion by cleaving the peptide bonds, particularly those involving the aromatic amino acids. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The parietal (oxyntic) cell is the engine of the gastric acid secretion. It is a large, triangular cell, packed with mitochondria, with a unique, specialized intracellular membrane system, the tubulovesicles. In the resting state, the H+/K+-ATPase (the proton pump) molecules are sequestered in the membranes of the cytoplasmic tubulovesicles. Upon stimulation, the tubulovesicles fuse with the apical canalicular membrane, massively expanding the secretory surface area and inserting the proton pumps into the membrane. The H+/K+-ATPase actively transports one hydrogen ion out of the parietal cell into the gastric lumen, in exchange for one potassium ion into the cell. This is the primary, active, ATP-consuming step. The transported potassium must be recycled back to the lumen via the apical K+ channels, otherwise the pump would stop for lack of substrate. The chloride ion exits the cell into the lumen via the apical chloride channel (the CFTR channel) and a parallel, independent chloride conductance. The basolateral membrane of the parietal cell possesses a Cl-/HCO3- exchanger (the anion exchanger 1, AE1, the band 3 protein). The bicarbonate that is generated inside the cell, when the carbon dioxide and water combine to form carbonic acid (catalyzed by the carbonic anhydrase II), is transported out of the cell across the basolateral membrane in exchange for the chloride that is needed for the secretion. This exit of bicarbonate into the blood produces the &quot;alkaline tide&quot;: a transient, postprandial rise in the systemic venous pH and the urine pH, which is a clinical sign of an active, functioning gastric acid secretion. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p>The three primary secretagogues that directly stimulate the parietal cell to secrete acid are: (1) Acetylcholine, from the vagal postganglionic ENS neurons, acting on the M3 muscarinic receptor, increasing the intracellular calcium. (2) Gastrin, from the antral G cells, acting on the CCK-B (gastrin) receptor, also increasing the intracellular calcium. (3) Histamine, from the ECL cells, acting on the H2 receptor, activating the adenylate cyclase and increasing the intracellular cyclic AMP (cAMP). The histamine pathway is the dominant and the most potent pathway. The three pathways potentiate each other: a small amount of histamine dramatically amplifies the response of the parietal cell to ACh and gastrin. This is the basis for the profound clinical efficacy of the H2 receptor antagonists. The gastrin is the endocrine mediator. The gastrin is released from the antral G cells in response to the luminal peptides and amino acids (especially the phenylalanine and the tryptophan), the antral distension, and the vagal stimulation (via the GRP, not ACh). The gastrin then travels through the bloodstream and stimulates the ECL cells in the corpus to release the histamine (the major pathway), and also directly stimulates the parietal cell. The acid secretion is inhibited by the somatostatin from the antral and the corpus D cells. The luminal acid (pH &lt; 3.0) directly stimulates the antral D cells to release the somatostatin, which paracrinally inhibits the neighboring G cells and the ECL cells, reducing the gastrin and the histamine release. This is the negative feedback loop that terminates the gastric phase of the acid secretion. The H. pylori bacterium disrupts this loop by producing the urease, which generates ammonia and neutralizes the acid in the microenvironment of the gastric antrum, impairing the D cell sensing of the acid, and leading to the hypergastrinemia and the acid hypersecretion. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2,3\">[1,2,3]</a></p><p>The acid secretion occurs in three overlapping phases. The cephalic phase is triggered by the sight, smell, thought, and taste of food, and is entirely vagally mediated. The gastric phase begins when the food enters the stomach. The gastric distension and the luminal peptides are the stimuli. The intestinal phase is a minor, stimulatory phase triggered by the partially digested protein in the duodenum (via the release of the entero-oxyntin from the duodenal mucosa), but it is rapidly overridden by the inhibitory duodenal brake. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The gastric acid has four essential functions. It converts the inactive pepsinogen to the active pepsin and provides the acid pH optimum for the pepsin activity. It denatures the ingested proteins, unfolding the globular tertiary structure and exposing the peptide bonds to the proteolytic attack. It kills the vast majority of the ingested bacteria and other microorganisms, providing a critical innate immune barrier to the intestinal tract. It facilitates the absorption of the dietary non-heme iron by reducing the insoluble ferric iron (Fe3+) to the absorbable ferrous iron (Fe2+). The loss of the gastric acid (achlorhydria, as in the autoimmune atrophic gastritis or the chronic PPI therapy) impairs the iron absorption and increases the risk of the enteric infections. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The parietal cell also secretes the intrinsic factor, a 60-kDa glycoprotein that binds the vitamin B12 in the stomach and protects it from the digestion. The intrinsic factor-B12 complex is then absorbed in the terminal ileum by the cubilin receptor. The intrinsic factor is the only essential secretion of the stomach; its loss (the pernicious anemia) is lethal without the vitamin B12 replacement, but the loss of the acid alone is not life-threatening. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"33-pancreatic-exocrine-secretion\">3.3 Pancreatic Exocrine Secretion</h3><p>The exocrine pancreas secretes approximately 1200–1500 mL of a clear, isotonic, alkaline (pH 8.0) pancreatic juice per day. The juice contains the complete set of digestive enzymes required for the intraluminal digestion of all three macronutrients, and the bicarbonate that neutralizes the gastric acid. The pancreatic secretion is the product of two distinct functional units: the acinar cells, which secrete the enzymes, and the ductal cells, which secrete the water and the bicarbonate. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The acinar cells are the classical, polarized, protein-secreting cells. They synthesize, package in the zymogen granules, and secrete by the regulated exocytosis the pancreatic digestive enzymes. The proteases are synthesized and stored as the inactive zymogens (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, procarboxypeptidase A and B). The enteropeptidase (enterokinase), a serine protease constitutively expressed on the duodenal enterocyte brush border, is the physiological activator. It cleaves the trypsinogen to the trypsin. The trypsin then autocatalytically activates more trypsinogen, and also activates all the other zymogens. This two-step, extraluminal activation is the critical safeguard that prevents the autodigestion of the pancreas. The other key enzymes are the pancreatic alpha-amylase (for the starch), the pancreatic lipase (the essential, rate-limiting enzyme for the triglyceride digestion), the colipase, the phospholipase A2 (activated by the trypsin), and the cholesterol esterase. The acinar cell is protected from the premature, intracellular activation of the zymogens by the physical separation of the zymogen granules and the lysosomes, the packaging of the enzymes as the inactive zymogens, and the presence of the pancreatic secretory trypsin inhibitor (PSTI) in the zymogen granule. The acute pancreatitis is initiated by the premature, intracellular activation of the trypsinogen to the trypsin, which then activates the other enzymes, and the autodigestion of the gland. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The ductal cells (the centroacinar and the intralobular duct cells) secrete a large volume of an isotonic, bicarbonate-rich fluid. The bicarbonate is actively secreted into the lumen against a large concentration gradient (the luminal bicarbonate concentration reaches 140 mM, compared to 25 mM in the plasma). The mechanism is coupled to the chloride secretion via the CFTR chloride channel, and the Cl-/HCO3- exchanger on the apical membrane. The secretin, released from the duodenal S cells in response to the luminal acid, is the primary and the most potent agonist of the ductal bicarbonate secretion. The secretin binds to its receptor on the ductal cell, activating the adenylate cyclase, and generating the cAMP, which opens the CFTR channel. The failure of this mechanism is the basis of the pancreatic insufficiency in the cystic fibrosis. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The phases of the pancreatic secretion are analogous to the gastric secretion. The cephalic phase is vagally mediated. The gastric phase is driven by the gastrin and the gastric distension. The intestinal phase is the dominant phase, accounting for 70–80% of the postprandial secretion, and is mediated by the secretin and the CCK. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"34-biliary-secretion\">3.4 Biliary Secretion</h3><p>The liver produces approximately 500–800 mL of bile per day. The bile is both a digestive secretion and a vehicle for the excretion of the waste products (the bilirubin, the cholesterol, the xenobiotics, and the heavy metals). The hepatocytes synthesize the primary bile acids (cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid) from the cholesterol, in a multi-step enzymatic pathway. The rate-limiting enzyme is the cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1). The bile acids are then conjugated with the amino acids glycine or taurine, which lowers their pKa, making them fully ionized (the bile salts) at the pH of the bile. The bile salts are actively transported across the hepatocyte canalicular membrane by the bile salt export pump (BSEP, ABCB11). The phospholipids (lecithin) are transported by the MDR3 (ABCB4), and the cholesterol by the ABCG5/G8 heterodimer. The canalicular bile is an ultrafiltrate of plasma, isotonic, with a bile acid concentration of approximately 50 mM. As it flows through the bile ducts, the cholangiocytes secrete a bicarbonate-rich, watery fluid, stimulated by the secretin and the VIP. The gallbladder concentrates the bile 10–15 fold by the active absorption of sodium and chloride (via the apical Na+/H+ and Cl-/HCO3- exchangers) and the passive, osmotically driven absorption of water. The gallbladder epithelium also secretes mucus. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The bile is stored and concentrated in the gallbladder during the fasting state. After a meal, the CCK from the duodenal I cells stimulates the gallbladder smooth muscle to contract and the sphincter of Oddi to relax, delivering the concentrated bile into the duodenum. The bile acids are the essential detergents that emulsify the dietary fat, forming the mixed micelles that solubilize the lipid digestion products and the fat-soluble vitamins, enabling their absorption. The bile is also the primary route for the elimination of the excess cholesterol. The bile acids are efficiently conserved by the enterohepatic circulation. Approximately 95% of the secreted bile acids are reabsorbed by the active, sodium-dependent transport (the apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter, ASBT, SLC10A2) in the terminal ileum. The absorbed bile acids return to the liver via the portal vein, are extracted by the hepatocytes (via the sodium-taurocholate cotransporting polypeptide, NTCP, SLC10A1), and are resecreted into the bile. The bile acid pool (approximately 3–5 grams) cycles 6–10 times per day. The small percentage of the bile acids that escapes the ileal absorption enters the colon and is deconjugated and dehydroxylated by the colonic bacteria to form the secondary bile acids (deoxycholic acid and lithocholic acid). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"35-intestinal-secretion\">3.5 Intestinal Secretion</h3><p>The small and large intestinal mucosa secrete a large volume of water and electrolytes, primarily from the crypt cells. The intestinal secretion is essential for the maintenance of the fluidity of the chyme, which permits the mixing, the diffusion, and the absorption of the nutrients. The secretion is driven by the active, electrogenic transport of chloride from the blood into the lumen. The chloride is taken up by the crypt enterocyte across the basolateral membrane by the sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter (NKCC1). The chloride then exits the cell across the apical membrane via the CFTR chloride channel. The sodium and the water follow the chloride passively through the paracellular pathway, driven by the electrochemical and osmotic gradients. The cAMP and the cGMP are the intracellular messengers that open the CFTR channel. The cholera toxin, the E. coli heat-stable toxin, and the VIP all cause a profound, secretory, watery diarrhea by irreversibly increasing the cAMP or the cGMP, keeping the CFTR channel locked in the open state. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p>"},{"معرّف":"3-digestion","عنوان":"3. Digestion","html":"<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p><p>Digestion is the chemical and mechanical breakdown of the complex macromolecules of food (carbohydrates, proteins, and fats) into their simplest constituent monomers—monosaccharides, amino acids and di-/tripeptides, and free fatty acids and monoglycerides—that are small enough to be transported across the intestinal epithelium. This process is accomplished by the sequential, coordinated action of luminal enzymes secreted by the salivary glands, stomach, and pancreas, and of brush border enzymes anchored to the microvillus membrane of the intestinal enterocyte.</p><h3 id=\"mechanisms-of-digestion\">Mechanisms of Digestion:</h3><ul><li><strong>Carbohydrate Digestion:</strong></li><li>The average Western diet contains approximately 300 g of carbohydrate per day, predominantly as starch (plant-derived polysaccharide of amylose [linear alpha-1,4 glucose polymers] and amylopectin [branched alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6]), sucrose (disaccharide of glucose-fructose), and lactose (disaccharide of glucose-galactose). Carbohydrates that are not digested are dietary fiber (cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins), which are resistant to human enzymes and pass to the colon for bacterial fermentation.</li><li><em>Luminal phase</em>: Alpha-amylase, produced by the salivary glands and the pancreas, cleaves the internal alpha-1,4-glycosidic bonds of starch, producing maltose (glucose-glucose disaccharide), maltotriose (glucose trisaccharide), and alpha-limit dextrins (branched oligosaccharides containing the alpha-1,6 branch points that alpha-amylase cannot cleave). Salivary amylase is largely inactivated by gastric acid, though some activity persists within the interior of the food bolus. Pancreatic alpha-amylase is the dominant enzyme.</li><li><em>Brush border phase</em>: The disaccharides and oligosaccharides must be hydrolyzed to their constituent monosaccharides at the brush border by specific enzymes that are integral membrane glycoproteins anchored in the enterocyte apical membrane, with their catalytic site protruding into the glycocalyx:<ul><li><em>Lactase</em>: Cleaves lactose to glucose and galactose. Lactase activity is high in infancy and declines after weaning in most of the world&#39;s population (lactase non-persistence), leading to lactose intolerance in adulthood. The exception is Northern European and some African pastoralist populations with a lactase persistence mutation in the MCM6 gene enhancer region.</li><li><em>Sucrase-isomaltase</em>: A single polypeptide chain with two catalytic domains. Sucrase cleaves sucrose to glucose and fructose. Isomaltase cleaves the alpha-1,6 bonds of alpha-limit dextrins (the isomaltase activity).</li><li><em>Glucoamylase (maltase)</em>: Cleaves the alpha-1,4 bonds of maltose and maltotriose, releasing glucose.</li><li><em>Trehalase</em>: Cleaves the disaccharide trehalose (found in mushrooms and insects).</li><li>The monosaccharides glucose, galactose, and fructose are now ready for absorption. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></li></ul></li><li><strong>Protein Digestion:</strong></li><li>Dietary protein intake is approximately 70-100 g/day. Endogenous proteins from desquamated enterocytes and secreted enzymes add another 30-50 g/day.</li><li><em>Gastric phase</em>: The gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen, which is activated by gastric acid to pepsin. Pepsin is an endopeptidase that cleaves peptide bonds on the amino side of aromatic amino acids (phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan), liberating large peptides and some free amino acids. Pepsin contributes only 10-15% of total protein digestion, which is why total gastrectomy does not cause significant protein malabsorption. The stomach&#39;s primary contribution is the grinding and trituration of solid protein into small particles that can be attacked by pancreatic enzymes.</li><li><em>Pancreatic phase (duodenal and jejunal lumen)</em>: The pancreatic acinar cells secrete a battery of proteases as inactive zymogens. Trypsinogen is activated by enteropeptidase (enterokinase), a serine protease constitutively expressed on the duodenal enterocyte brush border. Enteropeptidase cleaves an N-terminal hexapeptide from trypsinogen, forming active trypsin. Trypsin then autoactivates more trypsinogen and activates all other zymogens: chymotrypsinogen to chymotrypsin, proelastase to elastase, and procarboxypeptidase A and B to carboxypeptidase A and B. These enzymes have different peptide bond specificities and together degrade proteins into a mixture of free amino acids (40%) and oligopeptides of 2-6 amino acids (60%).<ul><li><em>Trypsin</em>: Cleaves peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of basic amino acids (arginine, lysine).</li><li><em>Chymotrypsin</em>: Cleaves peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of aromatic amino acids (phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan).</li><li><em>Elastase</em>: Cleaves peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of small, non-polar amino acids (alanine, glycine, serine).</li><li><em>Carboxypeptidase A and B</em>: Exopeptidases that cleave the C-terminal amino acid from the peptide chain.</li></ul></li><li><em>Brush border phase</em>: The enterocyte brush border contains peptidases (aminopeptidase N, dipeptidyl peptidase IV) that further hydrolyze the oligopeptides to amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, which are then absorbed. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></li><li><strong>Lipid Digestion:</strong></li><li>The average Western diet contains 60-100 g of lipid per day, predominantly triglycerides (90%), with phospholipids, cholesterol esters, and free cholesterol. Fat digestion poses two unique problems: (1) fats are hydrophobic and form large oil droplets in the aqueous intestinal lumen, requiring emulsification to increase the surface area for lipase action; and (2) the digestive products (fatty acids, monoglycerides) are insoluble and must be solubilized in mixed micelles for absorption.</li><li><em>Emulsification</em>: In the stomach, the grinding action of the antrum mechanically breaks large fat droplets into a coarse emulsion (particle size 10-20 µm). In the duodenum, the coarse emulsion is stabilized and further dispersed by bile salts and phospholipids from bile. Bile salts adsorb to the surface of the lipid droplets, coating them with a negatively charged outer layer that prevents coalescence. The emulsion droplet diameter is reduced to 1-5 µm, increasing the total surface area available for lipase binding by approximately 1000-fold.</li><li><em>Enzymatic Hydrolysis</em>: Pancreatic lipase is the dominant and essential enzyme for triglyceride digestion. It is secreted in its active form, but its activity is lipid-water interface dependent. It binds to the surface of the emulsified lipid droplets. However, bile salts, which coat the surface of the droplets, physically displace pancreatic lipase. This is overcome by colipase, another pancreatic protein secreted as procolipase and activated by trypsin. Colipase acts as an anchor: it binds to the surface of the bile salt-coated lipid droplet and provides a binding site for lipase, tethering it to the interface. Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides at the sn-1 and sn-3 positions, yielding two free fatty acids and one 2-monoglyceride. Phospholipids are hydrolyzed by phospholipase A2 (secreted as a zymogen, activated by trypsin) at the sn-2 position, yielding a free fatty acid and a lysophospholipid. Cholesterol esters are hydrolyzed by cholesterol esterase (carboxyl ester lipase), which also has a broad specificity for other lipid esters. Lingual and gastric lipases, secreted by glands of the tongue and gastric chief cells respectively, are active at pH 3-6 and preferentially hydrolyze short- and medium-chain triglycerides. They contribute approximately 10-30% of total triglyceride digestion, and their activity is especially important in neonates and in pancreatic insufficiency.</li><li><em>Micelle Formation</em>: The products of lipid digestion—free fatty acids, 2-monoglycerides, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins—are poorly soluble in the aqueous intestinal lumen. They must be packaged into mixed micelles, the essential transport vehicles that ferry them across the unstirred water layer to the enterocyte brush border. Bile salts, at concentrations above their critical micellar concentration (2-5 mM, achieved in the postprandial duodenum), self-associate into mixed micelles with phospholipids and the lipid digestion products. The polar (hydrophilic) groups of the bile salts and phospholipids face outward, and the hydrophobic core accommodates the non-polar lipids. The mixed micelle is a disc-shaped particle approximately 4-6 nm in diameter. It is not absorbed intact; at the brush border, the fatty acids and monoglycerides partition out of the micelle, diffuse down a concentration gradient across the enterocyte apical membrane, and the bile salt micelle returns to the lumen to ferry another load. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></li></ul><p><strong>Disease Example of Digestion Failure:</strong></p><p><em>Chronic Pancreatitis with Exocrine Pancreatic Insufficiency</em>: Years of chronic alcohol consumption cause recurrent episodes of inflammation, leading to progressive fibrosis and destruction of the pancreatic acinar cells. The secretion of pancreatic lipase, proteases, and amylase is progressively reduced. When &gt;90% of the exocrine pancreas is destroyed, clinically significant maldigestion occurs. Fat malabsorption (steatorrhea) is the most prominent, because pancreatic lipase is the only enzyme that can hydrolyze triglycerides in significant amounts (unlike protein and carbohydrate, which have some gastric and brush border backup). The stool is pale, greasy, foul-smelling, bulky, floats, and is difficult to flush. The patient loses weight despite an adequate caloric intake. The diagnosis is confirmed by a low fecal elastase-1 (&lt;100 µg/g stool, a direct measure of pancreatic secretion that survives intestinal transit). Treatment is pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (pancrelipase, containing porcine-derived lipase, amylase, and protease in enteric-coated microspheres). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a></p>"},{"معرّف":"4-absorption","عنوان":"4. Absorption","html":"<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p><p>Absorption is the transepithelial transport of the end-products of digestion—monosaccharides, amino acids and di-/tripeptides, free fatty acids and monoglycerides—as well as water, electrolytes, and vitamins, from the intestinal lumen, across the enterocyte, and into the blood or lymph. The small intestine is the primary site of absorption. Its surface area, approximately 30-40 m² (the size of a tennis court), is amplified 600-fold by three levels of folding: the plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring), the villi, and the microvilli (brush border). <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><h3 id=\"mechanisms-of-absorption\">Mechanisms of Absorption:</h3><ul><li><strong>Carbohydrate Absorption:</strong></li><li>Glucose and galactose are absorbed across the enterocyte apical membrane by the sodium-dependent glucose transporter 1 (SGLT1, SLC5A1). This is a secondary active transport protein that couples the uphill transport of one glucose or galactose molecule into the enterocyte to the downhill movement of two sodium ions. The energy is derived indirectly from the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase, which maintains the low intracellular sodium concentration. Fructose is absorbed by facilitated diffusion via GLUT5 (SLC2A5), a sodium-independent transporter. All three monosaccharides exit the enterocyte across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion via GLUT2 (SLC2A2) and enter the portal venous system, which delivers them directly to the liver. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></li><li><strong>Protein Absorption:</strong></li><li>The enterocyte absorbs free amino acids and dipeptides/tripeptides via multiple, overlapping, sodium-dependent and sodium-independent transporters on the apical membrane. Free amino acids are absorbed by specific amino acid transport systems with differing specificities for neutral, basic, acidic, and imino amino acids. The majority of protein is absorbed as dipeptides and tripeptides via the hydrogen ion-coupled peptide transporter 1 (PepT1, SLC15A1). PepT1 uses the inwardly directed proton gradient (the acid microclimate at the brush border) to drive the uptake of di-/tripeptides. Once inside the enterocyte, the absorbed di-/tripeptides are cleaved to free amino acids by cytosolic peptidases. The free amino acids then exit the basolateral membrane via specific amino acid transporters and enter the portal venous blood. The capacity of this system is enormous; even large protein loads are completely absorbed. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></li><li><strong>Lipid Absorption:</strong></li><li>At the brush border, the free fatty acids and monoglycerides in the mixed micelle reach the enterocyte microenvironment. The acidic microclimate (pH 5.5-6.0) at the brush border surface causes the bile salts to become protonated and temporarily less soluble, facilitating the release of the fatty acids and monoglycerides to the enterocyte membrane. They diffuse across the apical membrane, partly by passive diffusion due to their lipid solubility and partly via protein-mediated transport (CD36, FATP4 for long-chain fatty acids). Short- and medium-chain fatty acids (C4-C12) are sufficiently water-soluble to bypass this system; they diffuse directly into the enterocyte and then directly into the portal blood, where they travel bound to albumin.</li><li>Inside the enterocyte, the long-chain fatty acids are bound by fatty acid-binding proteins (FABPs) and transported to the smooth endoplasmic reticulum. Here, they are re-esterified to triglycerides (via the monoglyceride pathway: 2-monoglyceride + fatty acyl-CoA → diglyceride → triglyceride, catalyzed by acyl-CoA:monoglyceride acyltransferase and acyl-CoA:diglyceride acyltransferase). The newly synthesized triglycerides, together with cholesterol esters, phospholipids, and fat-soluble vitamins, are packaged into chylomicrons. Chylomicrons are large lipoprotein particles (75-1200 nm) with a core of triglycerides and cholesterol esters, surrounded by a phospholipid monolayer and the apolipoprotein B-48. Chylomicrons are too large to enter the fenestrated blood capillaries. Instead, they are exocytosed from the basolateral membrane of the enterocyte into the central lacteal, the lymphatic capillary within the villus. The chylomicrons travel via the intestinal lymphatics, the cisterna chyli, and the thoracic duct, which empties into the systemic venous circulation at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. This route bypasses the liver&#39;s first-pass metabolism. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></li><li><strong>Water and Electrolyte Absorption:</strong></li><li>The small intestine absorbs approximately 8500 mL of fluid per day and the colon absorbs 1400 mL, leaving only 100 mL in stool. Water absorption is entirely passive, secondary to the active absorption of solutes (primarily Na+). Water follows the osmotic gradient generated by solute transport, moving across the tight junctions (paracellular pathway) and across the enterocyte membranes via aquaporin water channels.</li><li>Sodium absorption occurs by multiple mechanisms along the GI tract. In the jejunum, sodium is co-transported with glucose and amino acids (SGLT1, amino acid transporters) and with chloride. In the ileum, electroneutral NaCl absorption occurs via parallel Na+/H+ (NHE3) and Cl-/HCO3- (DRA, downregulated in adenoma) exchangers. In the colon, electrogenic sodium absorption occurs via the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC), stimulated by aldosterone, which drives the final desiccation of the stool. Chloride follows sodium passively or is exchanged for bicarbonate. Potassium is absorbed passively in the small intestine and secreted in the colon (via apical K+ channels) when the luminal electronegativity generated by ENaC-mediated sodium absorption favors K+ secretion. Active potassium secretion explains why severe diarrhea leads to hypokalemia. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></li><li><strong>Vitamin Absorption:</strong></li><li>Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are solubilized in mixed micelles and absorbed with dietary fat into the chylomicrons. Water-soluble vitamins are absorbed by specific transporters or passive diffusion. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) absorption is uniquely complex: (1) In the stomach, dietary B12, bound to food proteins, is released by pepsin and acid. The free B12 binds to salivary haptocorrin (R-binder), which protects it from acid degradation. (2) In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin, releasing B12. (3) Free B12 then binds to intrinsic factor (IF), a glycoprotein secreted by the gastric parietal cells. The B12-IF complex is resistant to proteolysis. (4) In the terminal ileum, the B12-IF complex binds to the cubilin receptor on the ileal enterocyte brush border, is endocytosed, and B12 is released into the portal blood bound to transcobalamin II. Disease at any of these steps—pernicious anemia (loss of IF), pancreatic insufficiency (failure to cleave haptocorrin), or terminal ileal disease or resection (loss of cubilin)—causes B12 malabsorption. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></li><li><strong>Iron Absorption:</strong></li><li>Dietary iron exists as heme iron (from animal myoglobin and hemoglobin) and non-heme (inorganic) iron. Heme iron is absorbed by heme carrier protein 1 (HCP1) on the duodenal enterocyte apical membrane. Inside the enterocyte, heme oxygenase releases free Fe2+. Non-heme iron exists predominantly as Fe3+ in the diet and must be reduced to Fe2+ by the brush border ferric reductase, duodenal cytochrome B (DCYTB). Fe2+ is then transported into the enterocyte by the divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1). Inside the enterocyte, iron has two fates: (1) it is stored bound to ferritin and lost when the enterocyte is sloughed; (2) it exits the enterocyte across the basolateral membrane via ferroportin 1, the only known iron exporter. For efflux, iron must be oxidized back to Fe3+ by the basolateral ferroxidase, hephaestin. Fe3+ then binds to transferrin in the blood for systemic transport. The liver</li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"part-vi-gut-microbiome","عنوان":"Part VI: Gut Microbiome","html":"<p>The human GI tract harbors a vast, complex, and dynamic ecosystem of microorganisms, collectively known as the gut microbiome. The microbiome consists of approximately 100 trillion bacteria, belonging to over 1000 different species, as well as the archaea, the fungi, the viruses (the bacteriophages), and the protozoa. The bacterial density and diversity increase progressively along the length of the GI tract. The stomach and the proximal small intestine, due to the gastric acid and the bile, are relatively sparsely populated (10³–10⁵ bacteria/mL). The distal ileum contains a transitional flora (10⁷–10⁸/mL). The colon is a massive, anaerobic bioreactor, with the bacterial density reaching 10¹¹–10¹² bacteria per gram of the luminal content, which is the highest microbial density recorded in any natural habitat on Earth. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The gut microbiome performs vital, essential metabolic functions that the human host has not evolved to perform. These include: (1) The fermentation of the undigested dietary carbohydrates (the fiber, the resistant starch, the non-absorbable oligosaccharides) to the short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs): the acetate, the propionate, and the butyrate. The butyrate is the primary and the preferred energy source for the colonocyte. It is essential for the colonic epithelial health and the mucosal barrier integrity. The SCFAs also act as the signaling molecules, binding to the G protein-coupled receptors (GPR41, GPR43, GPR109A) on the enteroendocrine cells and the immune cells, regulating the satiety, the glucose homeostasis, and the inflammation. (2) The synthesis of the vitamins, especially the vitamin K (menaquinone) and the B vitamins (biotin, folate, cobalamin). (3) The metabolism of the bile acids, converting the primary to the secondary bile acids. (4) The metabolism of the xenobiotics and the drugs. (5) The education and the maturation of the intestinal and the systemic immune system. The germ-free animals have profoundly underdeveloped GALT, the Peyer&#39;s patches, and the mesenteric lymph nodes, and they are highly susceptible to the infections. (6) The colonization resistance: the healthy, dense, diverse, indigenous microbiome is a formidable barrier that prevents the colonization and the overgrowth of the exogenous, potentially pathogenic bacteria (e.g., C. difficile, the multi-drug-resistant Enterobacteriaceae). The disruption of the microbiome by the broad-spectrum antibiotics is the primary precipitant of the C. difficile colitis. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p>"},{"معرّف":"part-vii-gut-brain-axis","عنوان":"Part VII: Gut-Brain Axis","html":"<p>The gut-brain axis is the bidirectional, continuous, highly integrated communication network between the GI tract and the central nervous system. It is the anatomical and the physiological basis for the Disorders of Gut-Brain Interaction (the Rome IV criteria). The axis comprises several parallel, interacting signaling pathways. The vagus nerve is the primary, fastest, neural conduit. The vagal afferent fibers, which outnumber the efferent fibers by a ratio of 9:1, carry the information about the luminal nutrients, the gut wall distension, the inflammation, and the microbiome metabolites to the brainstem. The brain then processes this interoceptive information and modulates the GI function via the vagal efferent and the sympathetic efferent pathways. The gut microbiome is a major node in this axis. The bacteria produce the neuroactive molecules (the serotonin, the dopamine, the GABA, the acetylcholine precursors), the metabolites (the SCFAs), and the inflammatory signals that directly influence the brain function, the mood, the anxiety, the stress response, and the behavior. The enteroendocrine cells release the hormones (CCK, GLP-1, PYY, ghrelin) that signal directly to the brain (via the vagus nerve and the area postrema) to regulate the appetite and the satiety. The immune system of the gut (the GALT) produces the cytokines that can signal to the brain in the context of the inflammation. The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, the central stress response system, directly modulates the gut motility, the secretion, and the permeability, and alters the composition of the gut microbiome. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p>"},{"معرّف":"part-viii-integrated-response-to-a-meal","عنوان":"Part VIII: Integrated Response to a Meal","html":"<p>The ingestion of a meal triggers a precisely timed, coordinated, integrated sequence of the secretory and the motor events across the entire GI tract. This is the integrated physiological response. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p>The cephalic phase begins before the food is swallowed. The sensory inputs (the sight, the smell, the thought, and the taste of the food) activate the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus. The efferent vagal impulses stimulate the salivary glands (the secretion of the saliva and the amylase), the gastric parietal cells (the secretion of the acid and the pepsinogen), the G cells (the release of the gastrin), the pancreatic acinar cells, and the gallbladder. The stomach relaxes, and the LES tone is briefly reduced.</p><p>The oral and the esophageal phase: the chewing and the swallowing deliver the bolus into the stomach. The gastric phase begins. The gastric distension and the presence of the peptides and the amino acids in the antrum stimulate the release of the gastrin. The gastrin and the vagal stimulation drive the massive secretion of the acid. The antral contractions grind and triturate the solids. The gastric emptying of the small volume of the liquid chyme begins.</p><p>The intestinal phase is the dominant, homeostatic phase that matches the rate of the nutrient delivery to the capacity of the intestine to digest and to absorb. As the acidic, fatty, hyperosmolar chyme enters the duodenum, the S cells release the secretin, and the I cells release the CCK. The secretin stimulates the pancreatic ductal cells to pour a large volume of the bicarbonate into the lumen, neutralizing the acid. The CCK stimulates the gallbladder to eject the bile and the pancreatic acinar cells to secrete the enzymes. The fat is emulsified and digested. The digestion of all the macronutrients proceeds rapidly. The CCK, the GIP, and the GLP-1, along with the neural reflexes, slow the gastric emptying (the duodenal brake). The nutrients are absorbed. As the nutrients reach the distal ileum and the colon, the L cells release the GLP-1 and the PYY, which promote the satiety and the meal termination.</p><p>After the meal is fully absorbed (approximately 4–6 hours postprandial), the fed motor pattern ceases. The plasma motilin level rises, triggering the Phase III of the migrating motor complex, which sweeps the residual debris through the gut, and the cycle is ready for the next meal. <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></p><p><strong>Sources</strong></p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a> Hall JE, Hall ME. Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology. 14th ed. Elsevier; 2021.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a> Barrett KE, Barman SM, Brooks HL, Yuan JXJ. Ganong&#39;s Review of Medical Physiology. 26th ed. McGraw-Hill; 2019.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a> Schubert ML, Peura DA. Control of Gastric Acid Secretion in Health and Disease. Gastroenterology. 2008;134<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-7\" data-مصادر=\"7\">[7]</a>:1842-1860.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a> Goyal RK, Guo Y, Mashimo H. Advances in the Physiology of Gastric Emptying. Neurogastroenterol Motil. 2019;31<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a>:e13546.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-5\" data-مصادر=\"5\">[5]</a> Camilleri M. Physiology of Human Gut Motility. Physiol Rev. 2019;99<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a>:1877-2018.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a> Rao SSC, Bharucha AE, Chiarioni G, et al. Anorectal Disorders. Gastroenterology. 2016;150<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a>:1430-1442.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-7\" data-مصادر=\"7\">[7]</a> Said HM. Intestinal Absorption of Water-Soluble Vitamins: An Update. Curr Opin Gastroenterol. 2015;31<a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a>:122-130.</p><p><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\">[8]</a> Duckworth CA, Burkitt MD, Williams JM, et al. Mechanisms of the Epithelial Response to Injury. In: Sleisenger &amp; Fordtran&#39;s Gastrointestinal and Liver Disease. 11th ed. Elsevier; 2021.</p><div class=\"كتلة تحقّق\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Verification footer</p><p><strong>Guidelines used:</strong> none — physiology is not guideline-governed [PK].</p><p><strong>Generated:</strong> 12 September 2026.</p><p><strong>Could not verify — read this before quoting:</strong> the source document carries numbered citation markers but <strong>no bibliography</strong>. The numbers resolve to nothing, and every quantitative value here is <strong><button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button></strong> until a source is attached.</p><p><strong>Confirm before clinical application:</strong> treat this file as revision notes. For any treatment decision go to the primary guideline, not to this page.</p></div>"}],"مصادر":[]});
